{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The paper asks which users engage in hostile political expression online. Little is known about the characteristics of those who produce such hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Hostility and toxicity permeate political debates on social media, but less is known about which users engage in hostile political expression online.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "It challenges the classical view that political engagement is purely positive for democracy. It asks whether hostile expression on X/Twitter is associated with political engagement and its antecedents, not only with deprivation or personality.", "quotes": [{"text": "While many early theories of political behavior highlighted the positive democratic role of political engagement, recent research suggests that such engagement may also be associated with more polarized and antagonistic behavior.", "page": 1}, {"text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "The paper also asks whether politically hostile users are more active and have greater potential reach on X/Twitter.", "quotes": [{"text": "Third, we examine whether politically hostile users are more active and have greater potential reach on X/Twitter, while avoiding claims about formal network influence or centrality.", "page": 2}]}], "central_claim": [{"claim": "Online political hostility is positively associated with indicators of political engagement and its antecedents. The strongest associations are for political interest, political knowledge and online participation.", "quotes": [{"text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the strongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "Hostility is therefore linked not only to marginalization or personality. It is also linked to forms of engagement usually seen as central to democratic citizenship.", "quotes": [{"text": "These findings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based explanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered central to democratic citizenship.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Engaged but hostile users do not just address fringe audiences. They post more and have more friends and followers, which may give them greater reach.", "quotes": [{"text": "Finally, the results show that engaged but hostile social media users do not simply cater to fringe audiences; they post more frequently and have more friends and followers.", "page": 1}]}], "evidence": [{"claim": "The data link YouGov panel surveys of American users to their scraped public X/Twitter posts over about two years. This yields 2,567 linked panelists and over 9 million posts.", "quotes": [{"text": "To obtain our data, we commissioned the survey agency YouGov to invite panelists from their standing web panel to participate in a series of panel surveys.", "page": 6}, {"text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period was 2567.", "page": 6}, {"text": "During this period, we managed to scrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists.", "page": 6}]}, {"claim": "The primary hostility measure is a word-embedding 'Super-Unsupervised' score. It was validated against hand-labelled posts, among other checks.", "quotes": [{"text": "Our key measure of online political hostility is based on a word embeddings approach termed the Super-Unsupervised (SU) approach [60].", "page": 6}, {"text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.", "page": 7}]}, {"claim": "AFINN sentiment and Perspective API toxicity serve as robustness measures of hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Specifically, to assess the robustness and generality of the findings, we also used two additional measures of online hostility or negativity.", "page": 8}]}, {"claim": "Survey measures cover education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge and offline and online participation. Affective polarization is measured with feeling thermometers toward party leaders.", "quotes": [{"text": "From the surveys, we obtained individual-level measures of respondents’ education, sense of internal efficacy, political knowledge, political interest, self-reported participation in offline settings, and self-reported participation in online settings.", "page": 8}, {"text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump, and Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling thermometer.", "page": 9}]}, {"claim": "The analyses combine pooled OLS with cluster-robust standard errors, random-intercept cross-lagged panel models and negative-binomial count models for activity and reach.", "quotes": [{"text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to account for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves.", "page": 10}, {"text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM).", "page": 10}, {"text": "The exploratory activity and reach models are an exception: these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are reported on the original count scale.", "page": 10}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "Engagement indicators show positive associations with political hostility, with standardized coefficients generally above 0.2. These associations are larger for the political-hostility measure than for general negativity or toxicity measures.", "quotes": [{"text": "In terms of magnitude, the standardized coefficients for the associations between indicators of political engagement and the measure of online political hostility are generally above 0.2.", "page": 11}, {"text": "The largest associations are generally observed for the primary measure of political hostility rather than for the more general AFINN or Perspective API measures.", "page": 11}]}, {"claim": "Associations are more often significant among users high in leader-directed affective polarization. However, formal interaction support is clearest for online and offline participation.", "quotes": [{"text": "for those low on affective polarization only four out of eighteen associations, across our three measures of online hostility, are significant, whereas for those high on affective polarization fourteen out of eighteen are significant.", "page": 13}, {"text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight remain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.", "page": 14}]}, {"claim": "Within-person increases in online engagement are followed by more hostility. There is weaker evidence of the reverse direction.", "quotes": [{"text": "within-person increases in online political engagement are followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures.", "page": 15}, {"text": "We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this pattern is weaker and less consistent.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "More hostile users post substantially more and have more followers.", "quotes": [{"text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above the mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the number of followers from around 220 to around 370.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Results hold when