y partly capture proximity to politically salient conflict language or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "The measure is not a measure of hate speech.", "quotes": [{"text": "We prefer to use the term “political hostility” rather than “political hate” since we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate speech” which we are not purporting to measure.", "page": 7}]}, {"claim": "Affective polarization is measured only toward party leaders, not toward parties or ordinary supporters.", "quotes": [{"text": "our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than toward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters, as is standard [22].", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "The Big Five controls do not test Dark Triad or sadism accounts, which were not measured. The study does not adjudicate between grievance and personality literatures.", "quotes": [{"text": "the utilized Big Five controls do not directly test personality-based accounts centered on more antisocial traits such as Dark Triad traits, sadism, or other antagonistic traits.", "page": 16}, {"text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures.", "page": 3}]}, {"claim": "The reach analyses do not show formal network influence, centrality or community structure.", "quotes": [{"text": "These results suggest greater activity and potential reach, but they should not be interpreted as evidence of formal network influence or centrality.", "page": 15}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors extend the revisionist perspective on the negative sides of political engagement, built on motivated-reasoning and polarization research, to online political hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Here, we extend and integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on online political hostility.", "page": 4}, {"text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "They build on classical theories of political engagement together with recent work on its darker sides, combining the two strands.", "quotes": [{"text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we find that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Relative to the grievance and personality explanations of online hostility, they offer a complementary, different explanation rather than adjudicating between them.", "quotes": [{"text": "Instead, it asks whether online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and its antecedents.", "page": 3}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "Classical political-behavior literature (Verba, Schlozman, Brady; Nie et al.) holds that engagement is a boon to democracy that equips citizens for respectful discussion. The paper disputes this by finding that engagement is associated with hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Within the classical literature, political engagement has been seen as a boon to democracy. Formal education and a strong sense of internal efficacy potentially equip citizens with the motivation and capacity to partake in respectful conversations about topics of political importance online.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "Almond and Verba argued that engagement must be tempered by a more parochial relationship to politics. The paper builds on this early recognition that engagement can go too far.", "quotes": [{"text": "In particular [20], argued that engagement needed to be tempered with a more parochial relationship to politics such that “the democratic citizen is called on to pursue contradictory goals: he must be active, yet passive; involved, yet not too involved; influential, yet deferential.”", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "The motivated-reasoning literature (Leeper and Slothuus; Taber and Lodge; Kam; Slothuus and de Vreese) finds that engaged and knowledgeable citizens process information in a more biased way. The paper builds on this.", "quotes": [{"text": "Importantly, this literature also provided evidence that biased information processing is further exacerbated among those high in political knowledge [48,49].", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "Grievance-based accounts (e.g., Pettigrew, Petersen et al., Bail) link hostility to marginalization and status threat. The paper argues hostility is not only tied to these factors but also to engagement.", "quotes": [{"text": "Socioeconomic and grievance-based accounts instead focus on marginalization, deprivation, status threat, and perceptions of being left behind", "page": 3}, {"text": "These findings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based explanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15].", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Siegel states that less is known about the actors who produce hate speech. The paper builds on this gap by studying the characteristics of hostile users.", "quotes": [{"text": "As [6, p. 60] succinctly summarizes: “Less is known about the actors in informal communities dedicated to producing harmful content, or the accounts that produce hate speech on mainstream platforms.”", "page": 3}]}, {"claim": "Prior work, notably Osmundsen et al. on misinformation sharing, applied the revisionist view to social media only in a limited way. The paper extends that view to hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Importantly, prior research has only to a limited extent applied this revisionist perspective om political engagement to politics on social media and mainly in the context of misinformation sharing [53].", "page": 4}]}]}}
